from neandertals to lucy  discoveries of ancient hominids give us  a window into the past  but it's been a challenge for  researchers to figure out exactly how we  evolved from our more distant ancestors  who lived over 7 million years ago  a recent review in science traces how  fossil apes have  bolstered our understanding of human  origins  and what steps we can take to learn even  more  some paleontologists have interpreted  living african apes  as time capsules of myosin apes that  roamed the earth from 5  to 23 million years ago but this limits  the narratives of how we came to be  there are four lineages of living apes  chimpanzees gorillas orangutans  and gibbons many other lineages of apes  thrive during the miocene  but around 8 to 9 million years ago the  climate shifted from  warm widespread tropical forests  to cooler fragmented habitats this major  climatic shift  likely caused the extinction of many  myosin ape lineages  the over-reliance on living apes to  study humans  has stunted evolutionary theories  including how humans evolved bipedalism  the ability to walk on two hind feet  initially  researchers thought that humans were  able to walk on land  due to an adaptation from living apes an  upright posture developed from hanging  on branches all day  but fossil apes like pirilopithecus  catalaunicas  discovered in spain lacked the long  hands and curved fingers  tailored to dangling from trees  still the structure of its body showed  it had erect posture  which could have helped it climb trees  vertically this suggests that upright  posture  could have been the starting point for  bipedalism  instead of hanging ability a theory that  could not have emerged from studying  living apes alone  combining our knowledge of living and  fossil apes  could spark a revolution in how we see  our evolution  one step is expanding paleontological  survey sites  many mid to late miocene apes have been  discovered throughout europe  but fewer have been uncovered from that  time period in africa  where limited road access and dense  forests cover potential study locations  scientists still hope to discover fossil  apes that are more closely related to  gorillas and chimpanzees  as well as those that survived the major  climatic shift  in the late miocene new analytical  techniques  also provide more insight into specimens  that were already discovered  currently scientists can analyze ancient  proteins from bone and teeth  up to 2 million years old this helped  unveil that gigantopithecus  a specimen thought to be an ancestor of  gorillas  was more closely related to orangutans  3d scans of fossils will add even more  dimensions to these cross species  comparisons  lastly tracking living apes motions with  cameras  instead of describing them will help  pinpoint the link  between their movement and physical  traits  these steps will bring us closer to  grasping the astounding diversity  of the miocene's planet of the apes  you
 

